## 2. Managed Kubernetes (EKS / GKE / AKS)

**Q: What's the difference between running Kubernetes yourself and using EKS/GKE/AKS?**
A: A managed service handles the control plane for you (upgrades, availability, etcd management) — you focus on your worker nodes and workloads instead of operating the hardest, most critical part of the cluster yourself.

**Q: Have you worked with EKS, GKE, or AKS specifically?**
A: Answer honestly. *"I haven't run a managed Kubernetes cluster hands-on yet, but I've provisioned and configured VMs and networking directly on both AWS and GCP, so I understand the underlying cloud primitives (VPCs, IAM, networking) these managed Kubernetes services build on top of. I'm actively looking to get hands-on with EKS or GKE next."*

**Q: What are node groups/node pools in managed Kubernetes?**
A: Groups of worker nodes with the same configuration (instance type, OS, scaling settings) — EKS calls them "node groups," GKE calls them "node pools." They let you run different workload types on differently-sized nodes within the same cluster.

